 "land_use": {
  "about": "How much of a place can be farmed at most with ancient methods. Rain-fed farming needs woodland for fuel and pasture for the plough oxen, so pre-industrial arable land rarely passed 30-50% of the land; irrigated valleys like the Nile's were sown almost wall to wall. Slopes take orchards, vines and terraces; the rest grazes. Rain-fed fields lie fallow every other year. The ancient ard struggles with heavy clay: its yield falls by heavy_clay_penalty on the heaviest soils until the mouldboard plough (a later technology).",
  "field_max": 0.4,
  "field_max_irrigated": 0.85,
  "terrace_share": 0.2,
  "fallow_rainfed": 0.5,
  "heavy_clay_penalty": 0.35
 }
}